  "sheetname": "GermanPronouns3Es",
  "title": "es — It",
  "description": "Practise changing the object with es: present, future and past.",
  "updatedDate": 20261015,
  "intro": "Every example uses Es as its subject, meaning it in this file. Es stands for a neuter noun. For these seeing and giving examples, imagine das Gerät: a robotic device that can recognize people and hand over a book. This is a referring pronoun, not the impersonal es in weather expressions. Use sich when the subject is also the object or recipient. Object es refers to another neuter thing; sich refers back to the device. Keep the subject fixed and vary whom the subject sees or to whom the subject gives the book. Sehen takes an accusative object. Geben takes a dative recipient plus das Buch as the accusative thing. The highlighted key is the object or recipient. English labels: (s) singular, (p) plural, (f) formal.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "es — changing the object",
      "sortorder": 1,
      "explanation": "Start with the present-tense accusative and dative lists, then compare the same object choices in Futur I and Perfekt. Short sentences omit final full stops.",
      "rule": "Es sieht + Akkusativ | Es gibt + Dativ + das Buch",
      "entries": [
        {
          "header": "sehen — Präsens — present",
          "kind": "pattern",
          "explanation": "The subject and verb stay fixed. Change the accusative object.",
          "pattern": "Es sieht + Akkusativ",
          "examples": [
            {
              "german": "Es sieht mich",
              "english": "It sees me",
              "focus": "mich",
              "note": "",
              "key": "mich"
            },
            {
              "german": "Es sieht dich",
              "english": "It sees you (s)",
              "focus": "dich",
              "note": "",
              "key": "dich"
            },
            {
              "german": "Es sieht ihn",
              "english": "It sees him",
              "focus": "ihn",
              "note": "",
              "key": "ihn"
            },
            {
              "german": "Es sieht sie",
              "english": "It sees her",
              "focus": "sie",
              "note": "Singular feminine: her.",
              "key": "sie"
            },
            {
              "german": "Es sieht es",
              "english": "It sees it",
              "focus": "es",
              "note": "A different neuter referent; use sich for itself.",
              "key": "es"
            },
            {
              "german": "Es sieht uns",
              "english": "It sees us",
              "focus": "uns",
              "note": "",
              "key": "uns"
            },
            {
              "german": "Es sieht euch",
              "english": "It sees you (p)",
              "focus": "euch",
              "note": "",
              "key": "euch"
            },
            {
              "german": "Es sieht sie",
              "english": "It sees them",
              "focus": "sie",
              "note": "Plural: them. Same German spelling as her; context distinguishes the meaning.",
              "key": "sie"
            },
            {
              "german": "Es sieht Sie",
              "english": "It sees you (f)",
              "focus": "Sie",
              "note": "Formal object or recipient: always capitalized; one or more addressees.",
              "key": "Sie"
            },
            {
              "german": "Es sieht sich",
              "english": "It sees itself",
              "focus": "sich",
              "note": "Reflexive reading: itself.",
              "key": "sich"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "header": "geben — Präsens — present",
          "kind": "pattern",
          "explanation": "The subject, verb and das Buch stay fixed. Change only the dative recipient. Ihm is translated as him here, but can also refer to a neuter noun.",
          "pattern": "Es gibt + Dativ + das Buch",
          "examples": [
